# Maps, zones and location records

A map shows where an entity is reported to be. A text record says what the marker stands for and how certain the location is. Start with the question the household is asking, such as which zone a device is reported in. If a list of room names answers it, use the list and leave out the map.

The task must still make sense when tiles, imagery or pointer gestures are not available. For how to word a person's tracked location, and how it differs from their sign-in, see [Presence, people and shared-screen privacy](presence-and-privacy.html).

The examples are local previews. The device records are invented, and the map data is real OpenStreetMap data for public places in Cardiff Bay. They do not change a Home Assistant setting or show that a real device moved.

## Scope and native ownership

The native Map card draws markers, accuracy circles and grouped markers. In a panel view it adds People, Devices and Zones tabs. This page keeps those functions and the provider's attribution. It does not add a civica map engine.

The examples show a documentation pattern. They do not show that a native map feature has been installed or tested. A project logo, household name or business identity belongs outside the plotted data, and it must not cover markers, labels, legends or attribution.

<h2 id="map-context">Title, source and observation context</h2>

Give a changing map a neutral title, such as “Where are the devices?” or “Reported locations”. A fixed title such as “Everyone is home” becomes false when the data changes. Near the map, say in text what is measured, the area covered and when it was observed.

Name the tracker that supplied the observation and the provider that supplied the background. They are different sources, and the background provider's attribution stays visible. Do not publish credentials, precise household coordinates or a private provider URL.

Give each record its own observation time when reports arrive at different times. A page refresh is not a new location fix. If you show a retained report or missing accuracy, say so. Do not present old coordinates as current because the background loaded.

<h2 id="map-composition">Map hierarchy, extent and scale</h2>

Put the locations and their labels first, and keep the surrounding map quiet. Keep the provider's place names, and do not cover or recolour them to look simpler.

- Label a small number of features directly. For several categories, put a short legend before the map, near its upper-left edge.
- Fit the first view to the locations the task needs. Keep the person's chosen view during ordinary updates. If a selected record is outside the view, say so and keep it in the list.
- Add a scale bar only when distance matters and the map can show a meaningful local scale. Do not add one to a diagram or a floor plan with no measured dimensions.
- Show a zone boundary only when the source supplies it. An accuracy circle is the uncertainty of a report. It is not an allowed area, a property boundary or proof that a device is in a particular building.

The working example uses a north-up Web Mercator view of Cardiff Bay with a metric ruler that follows the zoom. The device coordinates are invented. The 30-metre accuracy circle is calculated from the invented report, so it is not a measured location. The example uses no private address and no browser location.

<h2 id="map-colour">Colour, labels, selection and uncertainty</h2>

Choose each colour for its job. A colour that identifies a device is not a warning, even in a red theme. Use few colours, and give every meaningful category a letter, shape or direct label as well. Say in text what is selected and what is uncertain, as well as showing it.

The working example uses six map roles: land, water, nature, structures, text and markers, and the label surface. Vivid and Muted change only the strength of the water and nature colours, and Light and Dark each have their own pair. None of these change the geometry, names, observations or selection.

- The two markers use the strong text and marker colour, plus a circle or diamond and the letters A and B. The letters match the key and the table.
- A solid outer boundary means selected. A dashed outline means reported uncertainty.
- None of the colours expresses how severe a device problem is, or how precise a location is.

Meet 4.5:1 contrast for ordinary text and 3:1 for markers and boundaries that carry meaning. Test against the real background, because a pair that works on white can fail over imagery. In forced-colour mode, keep the shapes, letters and line styles. See [Colour roles and accessibility](../styles/brand-colour.html) for the rules.

For a map that shows a quantity, such as rainfall, decide what it means before choosing the palette. Use [Chart colour](../styles/chart-colour.html#choose-the-colour-mapping) for scales, units and missing values. Keep a weather or flood provider's own thresholds and warnings, and do not recolour them as theme accents. These examples show categorical locations and a missing background. They do not show a quantity map or claim that one has been contrast-tested.

<h2 id="map-records">Every record remains reachable</h2>

Name the set and count plotted, unlocated and withheld records separately, where disclosure is allowed. “2 of 3 devices have a reported location” says more than “2 devices found”. Do not put an entity with no coordinates at 0°, 0°, and do not drop it from the total without saying so.

Provide an HTML table or labelled list of the same records before any interaction. Include the entity, the reported place, the source, the observation time and the accuracy when supplied. Do not make an essential value depend on hovering or a map gesture. A list of names alone is not enough, because it leaves out the location and the uncertainty.

A native group of markers counts the records inside it, not necessarily people. Show the members of a group. Two devices at one point remain two records. Opening a record, filtering the map or selecting a marker must not request a new position or change permissions.

## States and content decisions

| Source condition | Show | Do not |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Located entity | Name, reported zone or location, and observation context | Treat the accuracy circle as a property boundary |
| Markers grouped | The group name and its members | Announce the group as one person |
| No location supplied | A location-not-available record, where the native view supports it | Place the entity at 0°, 0°, or call it away |
| Tiles unavailable | The text records and the map's own error | Suggest the entities have moved |
| Historical route or zone | The period or configured zone | Present a route as a current observation |

## Integration contract

1. Use the native provider and keep its attribution visible, including at phone widths. Do not crop it or cover it with floating controls.
2. Read latitude, longitude, accuracy and zone states from Home Assistant. Do not look up names or send location data to a new provider just to apply a theme.
3. Keep the person's chosen focus and zoom. A normal state refresh must not drag the map away from a selected record.
4. Editing a zone changes configuration and can affect automations. Keep looking at a zone separate from editing it, make the edit an explicit step that checks permissions, and label radius units.
5. For zone editing, offer an alternative to dragging, such as the native numeric configuration. If the native view has none, record that as a limitation.
6. Label the zoom, grouping and reset controls, and check that the keyboard can enter and leave the map without a trap. The native Map card names its buttons Toggle grouping and Reset focus.
7. Keep provider imagery and the pictures of people and manufacturers as they are. Do not recolour photographs to match a theme.

## Failure and recovery

- With no current tracker value, say the location is not supplied. Do not say the entity is away. A configured Home zone does not show that anyone is there.
- If location permission is denied, say which feature is affected. A theme cannot grant permission.
- On a shared display, use the household's chosen level of detail and audience. Keep public examples and screenshots free of real addresses and coordinates.

