## Integration acceptance

- Check the title, area covered, source, period and attribution at 320px and with enlarged text. A diagram marked “not to scale” must not suggest measured distance.
- Compare each marker and selection cue with its key and its row in the table. Check the plotted and unlocated counts, and read both kinds of record without JavaScript.
- Check marker, background and text contrast, shapes, labels and forced-colour output in all four map appearances. Test the selected marker and keyboard focus over real geography. A check of the palette alone does not show the rendered contrast.
- Test grouped markers, two entities at one point, missing coordinates and failed tiles separately.
- Test the controls and text alternatives by keyboard, by touch, with enlarged text and in dark mode.
- Check that opening the page or changing a palette triggers no location request, movement or new sharing.

Record the actual environment, versions, method and result using [Native task accessibility and evidence](native-task-accessibility.html). A source review or a local example does not show physical behaviour, screen-reader use or live compatibility.

## What the native Map card offers

Checked on 17 November 2026 against the [Map card](https://www.home-assistant.io/dashboards/map/) and [Map tiles](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/map_tiles/) documentation, and the Home Assistant frontend source at commit `b1164739c3a853a28ba1c425415e96a17eb6286e`.

- **Map style** offers Default, Neon, Colorful, Natural, Muted, Gray and Toner. You can also set `map_style` to a custom style with `colors` and `colors_dark`. The dark colours replace the light colours. They are not merged with them.
- **Theme mode** offers Auto, Light and Dark. `scale_ruler` turns the native ruler on or off.
- Map tiles supplies the base map from the OpenStreetMap Foundation's servers through Home Assistant, which proxies and briefly caches the requests. On devices that cannot show the detailed map, it shows a simpler map from the same servers.

This documentation example contacts OpenStreetMap directly. It does not copy the native proxy and does not change any Home Assistant setting.

## Data, renderer and licence notices

The working map uses **MapLibre GL JS 6.16.0** and **VersaTiles Style 7.0.1**, the versions in this repository's `package.json`. The local style uses the documented custom-map-style colour names for its six roles. It keeps OpenStreetMap names and context, sets labels in local Arial at 14px or larger, and leaves out point-of-interest icons and labels that the example does not need. Changing the appearance keeps the geographic view and the device records. This is a documentation choice. It is not the style that native Home Assistant uses.

Map data stays **© OpenStreetMap contributors**, under the [Open Database Licence terms](https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright). The attribution stays visible in and below the working map. The [renderer and style-builder licence notices](../reference/map-demo-licenses.txt) ship with the example. The device records, key symbols and fallback symbol are original project content. Their provenance and alternative-text decisions are in `docs/data/map-design-examples.yml`.

The [OpenStreetMap provider configuration](../reference/map-example-provider.json) is separate from the bundled renderer. The example uses the OpenStreetMap Foundation's Shortbread vector tile service only after you select **Load OpenStreetMap**. It sends a normal browser Referer and follows the browser cache headers. It does not prefetch a region, create an offline archive, use a caching proxy or request a person's location. It asks only for the tiles in the bounded Cardiff Bay view. Read the [OSMF vector tile policy](https://operations.osmfoundation.org/policies/vector/) before changing that use. The provider may be unavailable, and the records stay usable if it is. The public repository gives the project's contact route.

These examples show a reusable documentation pattern. Native use, full screen-reader use and real-device compatibility need their own evidence.
